WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.220 --> 00:00:03.060
There is an old saying that "it's too cold to snow".

00:00:03.060 --> 00:00:07.299
Now a lot of people will say, that's obviously rubbish,
it can snow at any temperature.

00:00:07.300 --> 00:00:11.900
which, okay, is sort of true.
Let's rule out some technicalities

00:00:11.900 --> 00:00:15.960
to start off with. First of all, we are talking
about snow made of water here,

00:00:15.960 --> 00:00:17.440
not the methane snow

00:00:17.440 --> 00:00:21.960
that you might get on Titan or some other planet.
And of course, at cryogenic temperatures

00:00:21.960 --> 00:00:23.180
the kind of temperatures where

00:00:23.180 --> 00:00:27.750
oxygen and nitrogen become liquid, it's
certainly too cold to snow unless you start

00:00:27.750 --> 00:00:31.760
having a very strange definition of snow.
We are talking about the regular

00:00:31.760 --> 00:00:35.700
planet Earth, ice water snow, like that's
suddenly started falling

00:00:35.700 --> 00:00:38.930
just here. As air gets colder, it has much less

00:00:38.930 --> 00:00:42.640
capacity to hold water vapor.
It gets saturated

00:00:42.640 --> 00:00:47.270
far far quicker. That's because when it's
cold, the individual water molecules

00:00:47.270 --> 00:00:48.649
are much more likely

00:00:48.649 --> 00:00:53.180
to condense into liquid and much less
likely to evaporate

00:00:53.180 --> 00:00:57.920
out into vapour. So cold air is
much more likely to have already

00:00:57.920 --> 00:01:01.850
gotten rid of its water by forming
clouds somewhere else over there.

00:01:01.850 --> 00:01:04.900
So cold air is drier, and therefore

00:01:04.900 --> 00:01:09.490
it's too cold to snow. But there are
a few other factors involved too.

00:01:09.490 --> 00:01:14.600
The temperature down here -
while it's quite cold at the minute -
is not the same as up there.

00:01:14.600 --> 00:01:18.480
Well, obviously, it's much colder up there. But they don't normally follow each other in lockstep.

00:01:18.490 --> 00:01:22.700
Just because it's cold and dry down here
doesn't mean the ice crystals aren't forming

00:01:22.700 --> 00:01:27.040
up there in the clouds. And that's if there is
any cloud up there, of course.

00:01:27.040 --> 00:01:31.130
That's the other problem. Let's not
forget that snow can only form

00:01:31.130 --> 00:01:34.210
if there's some cloud to form it in.
Clouds are also

00:01:34.220 --> 00:01:38.360
really good at reflecting the heat
from the earth back at the surface.

00:01:38.360 --> 00:01:40.800
If there's no cloud, sure, there definitely won't be snow,

00:01:40.800 --> 00:01:42.800
but it's also going to be much colder

00:01:42.800 --> 00:01:45.700
because the heat isn't trapped
below that cloud layer.

00:01:45.700 --> 00:01:49.729
So the cause and effect is sort of the
wrong way round there, but you can see

00:01:49.729 --> 00:01:53.310
where the old wives' tale comes from.
Yes, for those reasons,

00:01:53.310 --> 00:01:56.450
as the temperature drops further and
further and further snow

00:01:56.450 --> 00:02:00.939
becomes less and less and less likely.
There is some truth to the old saying.

00:02:00.939 --> 00:02:03.920
It can be too cold to snow.

00:02:03.920 --> 00:02:08.250
It is very cold here right now, though,
and I am going back inside.

00:02:09.180 --> 00:02:11.480
Aagh! It's down the back of my neck!

00:02:12.860 --> 00:02:14.860
[Translating these subtitles? Add your name here!]

